{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>You may slaughter and eat flesh.</b> Rashi's comment: there is no permit for shearing them — meaning, one who shears transgresses 'do not shear,' but the wool is permitted for benefit, as we say there: the wool of a firstborn that was placed in a window and the animal died is permitted. And Rashi further comments: there is no permit for their fat. Here it implies that fat is forbidden for benefit by Torah law, but in the passage of sacrilege we say: fat of consecrated animals and the egg of turtledoves — one may not derive benefit from them nor commit sacrilege with them. And since no sacrilege applies, it follows there is no Torah prohibition but only a rabbinic one. One may answer: if it was lactating at the time it was consecrated, the fat is forbidden even by Torah law; but when it is not lactating at the time of consecration, it is forbidden only by rabbinic law." "<b>The impure and the pure together may eat it.</b> Whether impure or pure, they are permitted to eat it — unlike before a blemish befell it, where an impure person who eats it is liable to excision."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:52:36.105293039Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}